      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Kaskaskia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village became Illinois Territory's first capital on February 3, 1809, before moving to Vandalia in 1819.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaskaskia%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A 650‑pound bronze bell, dubbed the “Liberty Bell of the West,” was gifted to Kaskaskia’s church by Louis XV in 1741.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Kaskaskia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Fort de Chartres, built in the 1750s and abandoned in 1771, still stands as a reconstructed garrison north of the village.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
